{-
For example, you could have a test that checks the `remove` method. The assumptions could be that the list contains the specified element, the specified element is a valid index in the list. The preconditions could include the number of elements in the list, whether or not the list contains a particular item, and whether or not a particular index is valid.

If the element is removed from the list, then the number of elements in the list should be reduced by 1. If the list no longer contains the element that was removed, that could be a problem. Similarly, if the element isn't removed because it doesn't exist in the list (maybe because of an invalid index), that could also cause problems. 

The assertions could then check if the list count has decreased by 1, if the list no longer contains the specified element, and that the index the element was removed at doesn't exist in the list anymore. If all these conditions are met, the mutator method passed the test.

This approach can help to provide some certainty about the expected behavior of a mutator method, even when you don't actually have explicit unit tests that cover every possible input and output.
-}

{-
## Summary

In the tests we used, the purpose was to cover preconditions, to observe the execution of the mutator method, and to examine postconditions.

These kinds of tests are a good fit for mutator methods (methods that manipulate state), as they give us a way to ensure that methods behave as intended. In other words, they let us know whether our methods are working correctly according to the contract that we have defined.

Remember, when you use these tests, the most important thing is to carefully choose your assumptions and assertions. A good test is one that covers a wide range of precondition inputs and verifies a wide range of postconditions. However, this is not always possible, so you need to take into account the complexities of the implementation and potential issues.

Pex also enables you to test the non-deterministic behaviors of your code, which makes it a powerful tool for fuzzing your code.

As you continue to learn, you might want to explore other forms of testing. You could also apply a different style of testing that better fits your goals. In addition to these tests, you could also use unit tests to verify small functional units of your code and integration tests to verify how your code works in concert with other components of your system. Remember, it's important to know when to test, where to test, and how to handle the things that make testing difficult, like non-deterministic--------------------
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
            Assert.AreEqual(x, arrList[index]);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListIndexOfX, index);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ is x inserted at its right index 
             Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, index);
	     
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ is x contained in arrList after insertion 
             Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
``` 

Please take note that `@Arrange`-`@Assert`-`@Act` block in tests are used to organize test code into three clear sections. If you want to perform an operation and observe the state of the system, typically this action is in the `@Act` section. A `@PostAssert` block is used to observe state after the assertions were evaluated.

Please keep in mind that the contract tests and other test types are only applicable if an appropriate framework is in place. This may include a testing framework for checking preconditions, postconditions, and invariants. Additionally, some concepts in this description may not be applicable if the code under test has no public behavior (i.e., no methods). 

# Summary

In conclusion, automated software testing such as contract testing is a great way to validate that the system behaves correctly under a specified set of circumstances. The process is typically divided into three main steps: planning, execution, and analysis. The purpose of automated testing is two-fold: not to test, but to verify that the testing phase is complete. Regardless of the specific tool or platform being used, it is paramount to properly structure and write test cases to maintain the intended validation process. Furthermore, it is important to maintain the highest level of documentation regarding pre-conditions, post-conditions, and invariants. This allows for future debugging or enhancing efforts to be minimized, thereby improving the overall system's quality. 

If you found this information helpful or have any additional questions, feel free to ask.

