## Property\[T\] - \[PexProperty\]  
  
Property[T\] provides the ability to create methods that rely on properties of the tested class instance. It generates test data dynamically using a provided type argument. To use with--------------------	    // and less than or equal to arrList.Count
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListIndexOfX <= arrList.Count);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index of x after insertion is equal to index
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_index);

	   // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ LastIndex of x after insertion is equal to index
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_index);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ arrList.Contains(x) is true
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```
The PexObserve statements help to visualize the input parameters and output results of the method under test. 

### Invariants
Invariants are used to validate that certain requirements held to be true prior to execution of an assertion hold to be true post execution. For example, 
```csharp
public void Add_insert_maintains_count_invariants(ArrayList arrList,int x,int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count);
    int oldSize = arrList.Count;
    int oldCapacity = arrList.Capacity;
    arrList.Insert(index, x);
    int newCount = arrList.Count;
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("oldSize", oldSize);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("newCount", newCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("oldCapacity", oldCapacity);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("arrList.Capacity", arrList.Capacity);
    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that " newCount >  oldSize"
    Debug.Assert(newCount > oldSize);
    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that "newCount == oldCount+1"
    Debug.Assert(newCount == oldSize + 1);
    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that "arrList.Capacity > oldCapacity"
    Debug.Assert(arrList.Capacity > oldCapacity);
}
```
In the above method, I have used the "Debug.Assert( condition )" to check certain inequalities to maintain the count invariants before and after addition of an element in ArrayList. The conditions are to check if new count is greater than the old count, new count is exactly old count + 1 and the capacity of the ArrayList is greater than its current capacity.

## Code Contracts and Expression Level Assertions: Best Practices

1) Include all needed inputs and outputs in method's parameters, so that Pex can generate meaningful test cases.

2) The invariants should be based on pure function properties, i.e., the effect of an operation on the state of an object should not depend on the state of other objects in the system.

3) Avoid using "volatile" fields, as Pex doesn’t handle these fields correctly.

4) Avoid testing the same function with different types as it increases the number of test cases drastically and it will be hard to distinguish which of the inputs has caused the failure.

5) Test private methods individually as they provide value only when used by class methods and by calling Pex, testing of multiple cases might not be needed.

6) When testing a method that can throw exceptions, consider what type of exceptions your method can throw and what will happen if it does throw an exception.

7) To test performance critical methods, you can use fuzz testing (Testing with varied inputs) to generate input data that simulates high usage. However, for typical real-world usage, you should preferably limit the fuzzing to 1000 iterations.

8) Make use of PexAllocationFail method to test the scenarios where the creation of objects fails. This is an advanced usage and not needed for regular unit testing.

Please remember, any testing strategy should follow the golden rule: “Keep Testing Simple, Stupid”. Remember, automated unit tests are just as good as a human tester with a better understanding of the code.

