```

This test case generates a list of data and selects a value and an index where to insert it. After performing the insert operation on the ArrayList, it assesses the resultant state of the ArrayList - checking properties like `Count` and `Contains`. It also checks that inserted value remains at its right position and it does not occur earlier than the insertion point. Some properties of the list before and after the operation are presented for visualization. All conditions are asserted using `Debug.Assert(condition; message)` for additional clarity. It also uses `Assume.That(condition)` for input data precondition checking. It uses `AssumePrecondition` and `PexObserve` to observe and inspect the state of the arrayList before and after the operation.

Please note that this set of tests is an abstract implementation and may not compile or work perfectly out of the box. However, it can be used as a guide to build your own contract tests using Pex.

To test insert operation, the input (ArrayList, value to be inserted, and index) is selected for the action using [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute, and it makes assumptions on the pre-conditions using the Assume class. After the operation, it makes assumptions on the post-conditions. It uses Debug.Assert for assertion of post-conditions and PexObserve for visualization.

In conclusion, Pex provides an extensive framework by allowing you to generate random inputs to perform contract and behavior test cases on your .NET code. These tests help you not only to test your code in isolation but also to catch possible bugs and edge cases. Pex can generate a large set of test inputs and can be used to validate the behavior and contract of your code under different combinations of input parameters.

